title Fold Z -- the reap cluster, and the split that was only a word
prompt Autonomous loop -- take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, Standfast on reds first, council rota one doc, send as its own signed round
think Standfast first -- walked crux/REDS.md by opened-versus-closed row with a script rather than by eye. Every opened row carries a closure; %83 reads as prevention LANDED 20260817 rather than the word CLOSED, which is why a naive grep flags it. No open agent-closable red, so the line is clear.
think Council rota, lap index 26 of 27 -- deep-read active-designing/20260812-171050_the-1024-round-itinerary.md. Its filling law is the one this arc runs under: Lindy-first crux-first orders everything, reds preempt, a quest is filled rather than invented, and the gates are the fence.
think Ran tools/caravan_ladder_carry_witness.rish to let the fourth meter choose the crux by measurement -- reap_oldest at the head, 37 rungs by 44 lines, 1584 carried, exactly as the last lap predicted.
think Hashed every reap_oldest across caravan/*.rye rather than trusting the queue. Forty files hold the 44-line body; thirty-seven are pub and three are not, and the three are byte-for-byte identical otherwise. A third kind of absentee -- a family split by one word of visibility, which a meter keyed on text cannot help but read as two.
think Audited pub-ness before moving a line. Eleven of the thirteen reached symbols already stood public in all forty. The two that did not are reclaim_one and abandon_one, the private helpers the body calls -- so the fold could not happen without widening them.
think Hashed those two as well, which turned the lap. Each stands byte-identical in the SAME forty rungs, with the same three elder outsiders -- reclaim.rye, reckon.rye, abandon.rye. One body, two helpers, one cohort, one shared fossil record.
think Chose to lift the cluster whole rather than the family alone. It is one dependency closure found by one audit; splitting it into three laps would repeat the same widening and the same audit three times, and the round's own shape bounds it.
think Audited the helpers' own reach before appending -- max_in_flight, read_count, reclaim_refusal, reclaim_notes, abandon_refusal, abandoned_reach, abandon_note, abandoned_by -- all eight already public in all forty. So the widening stops at the two helpers and needs no accessor.
think Wrote the three harness bodies into caravan/ladder_checks.rye with every rung-owned reach seated through the rung handed in, and cohort, carry, and cycle reached from the harness's own imports the way every lifted body already does.
think Replaced the bodies by script, verifying each of the 120 bodies against its expected digest BEFORE touching it and refusing on any mismatch -- measurement before the edit rather than after.
obs The third kind of absentee is the gentlest one this arc has met. Fold R's two kinds were a body dated by a shape born after it and a tier owner reaching its own report directly; this one is neither. Nothing about the ladder differed at all -- only what a text-keyed meter could see.
obs Carrying families fell 707 -> 706, the first fall in this arc. Every fold before it traded one carrying family for another and left the count unmoved; this one traded four for three, because the visibility split was healed by the same act that lifted the body. The clearest way to fix a measurement is to remove the thing that made it wrong.
obs The new reading -- the widening a fold needs names the next fold. A body and the helpers it reaches enter the ladder together and settle together, so the reach graph is the queue read one step ahead of the meter. Fold Y predicted its own shape from the tier structure; this lap predicted the NEXT two families from the reach closure.
obs The carry meter's GREEN close and its three-windows line each still recited a number no assert in that file held. Freed both of their numbers rather than repinning them, which is REDS %110's rule applied where the rule came from -- the second lap running that this has been needed, which says the habit is in the prose rather than in one line.
